Yes — skb_header_pointer handles non-linear by skb_copy_bits fallback. So it returns NULL only when the requested bytes aren't in the skb at all (offset+len > skb->len). So it never returns NULL merely due to non-linearity; it copies into the stack buffer. So pattern B "NULL ignored" matters only when offset+len can exceed skb->len — i.e., missing length validation, not merely non-linear. That's the key insight: skb_header_pointer itself is non-linear safe. The seed bug is different: pskb_may_pull after obtaining pointer.

